---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import JsonLd from '~/components/common/JsonLd.astro';
import { SITE } from 'astrowind:config';

const metadata = {
  title: `UV Printing vs EVA Block Piecing — SUP Artwork Technology Comparison | ${SITE.name}`,
  description:
    'Technical comparison of UV digital printing and EVA color-block piecing for SUP board artwork. Durability, color fastness, cost, and application methods for each technology.',
  ignoreTitleTemplate: true,
};
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'UV Printing vs EVA Block Piecing — SUP Artwork Technology',
  description: metadata.description,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name },
  datePublished: '2026-07-27',
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <JsonLd data={jsonLd} />
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">
    <nav class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-8">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-sky-600">{SITE.name}</a><span class="mx-2">/</span><a
        href="/research"
        class="hover:text-sky-600">Research</a
      ><span class="mx-2">/</span><span class="text-gray-900">SUP Artwork Technology</span>
    </nav>
    <div class="mb-4">
      <span
        class="inline-block text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-sky-600 bg-sky-50 px-3 py-1 rounded-full"
        >Technology</span
      ><span class="ml-3 text-sm text-gray-400">6 min read</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">UV Printing vs EVA Block Piecing</h1>
    <p class="text-xl text-gray-500 mb-12 leading-relaxed">
      Two fundamentally different approaches to adding artwork to SUP boards — understanding their trade-offs is
      essential for choosing the right manufacturing method.
    </p>
    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none prose-headings:text-gray-900 prose-p:text-gray-600">
      <h2>UV Digital Printing</h2>
      <p>
        UV printing applies ink directly to the PVC or EVA surface using inkjet print heads, then cures it instantly
        with UV light. This allows full-color photographic-quality artwork with gradients, shadows, and fine details. UV
        inks are formulated with flexible polymers that can stretch up to 150% without cracking, accommodating board
        inflation and folding. The printing is performed on flat PVC sheets before assembly or on finished boards using
        3-axis print gantries.
      </p>
      <h3>Advantages</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Full CMYK color with no design complexity limits</li><li>Photographic quality with smooth gradients</li><li>
          Lower setup cost for custom runs
        </li><li>Fast iteration — digital file to printed board in hours</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Limitations</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Surface ink layer — vulnerable to abrasion from foot traffic</li><li>
          UV exposure can fade inks over 2-3 years
        </li><li>Stretchable inks are more expensive than standard UV inks</li><li>
          High-elongation areas (rail curves) may show micro-cracking over time
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h2>EVA Color-Block Piecing</h2>
      <p>
        Color-block piecing uses physically different colored EVA sheets that are die-cut and assembled into a seamless
        pad. The color is inherent to the EVA material — there is no ink layer to scratch, peel, or fade. CNC
        die-cutting achieves sub-millimeter precision for complex interlocking shapes.
      </p>
      <h3>Advantages</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Zero fading — colors are structural, not surface-applied</li><li>
          100% abrasion resistant under foot traffic
        </li><li>No cracking from inflation or folding</li><li>Lasts the lifetime of the board</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Limitations</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Limited to solid color blocks — no gradients or photographic detail</li><li>
          Higher die-cutting tooling cost per design
        </li><li>More complex assembly labor</li><li>Designs limited to interlocking geometries</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Hybrid Approach</h2>
      <p>
        Afarer uses a hybrid approach on many models: UV digital printing on the nose (where photographic detail matters
        and foot traffic is minimal) combined with EVA color-block piecing on the standing zone (where abrasion
        resistance is critical). This division of labor gives each technology responsibility for what it does best. For
        360-degree visual continuity, screen-printed rail bands extend the design around the board sides using
        specialized stretchable PVC inks.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
